Title: Flipped
Author: Gitish98
Draft date: May 2026
Genre: Psychological Thriller
Format: Short Film (~22 pages)
Contact:

===

FADE IN:

INT. SEMINAR HALL - NIGHT

Two thousand seats. Every one of them full.

The stage is minimal — a single spotlight, a single man. DR. BRIAN McGEE (52) moves through the light like he was born in it. No notes. No podium. Just a wireless mic clipped to the open collar of a shirt that cost eight hundred dollars to look like it didn't.

McGEE
(soft, certain)
The moment you've been waiting for your whole
life... is not a moment of triumph.

He lets that land. The room leans in.

McGEE (CONT'D)
It's a moment of surrender.

A WOMAN in the third row begins to cry. She doesn't cover her face.

McGEE (CONT'D)
You've spent thirty years — forty years — building
the version of yourself that could survive. And it
worked. You're here. You're alive.

He pauses.

McGEE (CONT'D)
But surviving isn't living. Is it.

Not a question. An offering. The room accepts it.

APPLAUSE builds from scattered agreement into something total — a standing ovation that begins before he's finished, because the audience wants to give him something.

McGee receives it with a slight bow. Grateful. Humble.

The camera finds one face in the crowd that isn't standing.

NADIA OSEI (34) sits with her hands in her lap, watching the stage with the focused stillness of someone reading a document. Not hostile. Not moved. Calibrating.

CUT TO:

INT. SEMINAR HALL - BACKSTAGE - CONTINUOUS

The noise of the ovation is muffled now. McGee walks off stage and the mask — not off, exactly, but resting. His ASSISTANT, DEREK (28, efficient, devoted), hands him a water.

McGEE
Numbers?

DEREK
Twenty-two hundred. Waiting list for the intensive
is up to four months.

McGEE
Move the top tier to six weeks. Anyone flagged
high on isolation metrics goes to the front.

He says this the way a logistics manager discusses shipping routes.

DEREK
The Chicago podcast is asking about a live taping.

McGEE
Tell them October. I want the new book out first.

He glances back toward the stage as the applause continues.

McGEE (CONT'D)
People don't want to be fixed.

He takes a sip of water.

McGEE (CONT'D)
They want to feel like someone finally understands
how broken they are.

He walks away. Derek follows.

CUT TO:

TITLE CARD: FLIPPED

CUT TO:

TITLE CARD: SIX WEEKS EARLIER

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

The office is engineered to feel like safety.

Warm amber light. No overhead fluorescents. Furniture chosen to suggest comfort without clinical coldness — a deep couch, a low table, two chairs angled toward each other rather than across from each other. A white noise machine hums softly outside the door.

There are no diplomas on the wall.

McGee sits in one of the chairs. Nadia sits across from him, straight-backed, coat still on. First session.

McGEE
You can take your coat off.

NADIA
I'm fine.

A beat. McGee notes it. Files it.

McGEE
Tell me why you're here.

NADIA
My brother died. Eight months ago.

McGEE
I'm sorry.

NADIA
It was a suicide.

She says it the way she says most things: precisely, with the air of someone who has decided not to be ambushed by a word.

McGEE
And you've been carrying that.

NADIA
Everyone carries it. I want to understand it.

McGEE
Those aren't the same goal.

She looks at him. Something in that response was faster than she expected.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Understanding can be a way of staying close to
something we're not ready to let go of.

He says it gently. He means it. This is what makes him good at what he does.

Nadia uncrosses her legs. Considers him.

NADIA
What do you think I'm not ready to let go of?

McGEE
I don't know yet.

(beat)

But I notice you haven't taken your coat off.

The ghost of something on Nadia's face — not quite a smile. Despite herself.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY (SESSION 3 — THREE WEEKS LATER)

Nadia's coat is on the hook by the door. Progress, or the appearance of it.

McGee is in the middle of a technique Nadia will later note in her journal as: *Retrospective Attribution Shift — Phase 2.*

McGEE
You mentioned Marcus called you the week before.
And you didn't call back right away.

NADIA
I was busy. I called him three days later.

McGEE
Mm.

NADIA
What?

McGEE
Nothing. I'm just —

(settling back)

What does it feel like, when you say that?

NADIA
What does what feel like?

McGEE
"I was busy."

A long beat.

Nadia's expression doesn't change. But something behind her eyes does.

She is watching him with sudden, total attention — the way you watch something when you realize you've been looking at it wrong.

McGEE (CONT'D)
(noticing nothing)
Take your time.

Nadia reaches into her bag. Takes out a small notebook. Writes something.

NADIA
Sorry. I just want to remember something.

McGEE
Of course.

He watches her write. He is curious but not concerned. He has never had a patient take notes before.

NADIA
(without looking up)
Can we go back? You were asking about the call.

McGEE
We can go wherever you need to go.

She looks up at him. Gives him the answer he's waiting for — just enough weight in it to confirm the reframe is landing.

NADIA
I think you're right. I think "busy" is a word
I use when I mean something else.

McGee nods, gently, encouragingly.

He doesn't see her write one more word in the notebook.

CLOSE ON: the notebook page. One word underlined twice.

*TECHNIQUE.*

CUT TO:

INT. NADIA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Books spread across a kitchen table. Graduate texts, dog-eared. A legal pad covered in her handwriting, organized into columns.

On her laptop screen: McGee's podcast, paused mid-episode. She's been through four episodes tonight — we can see the browser tabs.

She picks up her phone. Pulls up a contact: MARCUS. Doesn't dial it. Just looks at the name.

Sets the phone down. Goes back to the legal pad.

The column on the right is labeled: *PHASE 2 — RETROSPECTIVE ATTRIBUTION.*

Below it, a list of direct quotes from her sessions, matched against techniques from her textbook.

They match. Line by line. Precisely.

She sits with this for a moment.

Then she closes the textbook and opens a new document on her laptop. Begins to type.

CUT TO:

INT. COMMUNITY CENTER - EVENING

A support group. Ten chairs in a circle, nine of them occupied.

These are McGee's patients — various ages, various wounds. They speak with the particular fluency of people who have been given a shared vocabulary. The language of the process.

Nadia is here for the third time. Listening.

CAROL WHITMORE (47) is speaking. She's warm-faced, effortful, wearing a sweater with a small embroidered bird on it.

CAROL
I had a really hard week. Like, genuinely hard.
But Brian says the hard weeks are when the real
work is happening, so I'm trying to be grateful
for it instead of —

(small laugh)

— instead of just feeling terrible.

Scattered nodding around the circle. Murmurs of recognition.

NADIA
What made it hard?

The group looks at her. Nadia doesn't ask questions, usually.

Carol blinks, slightly caught off guard.

CAROL
I've been having these thoughts. At night mostly.
Really dark ones.

NADIA
What kind of thoughts?

CAROL
Brian says they're part of the process. That
the darkness is just the old self resisting.

NADIA
(carefully)
What do the thoughts feel like to you? Outside
of what Brian says about them.

A beat. Carol seems to try to locate that distinction.

CAROL
I mean... scary, I guess. But he says —

NADIA
You said scary first.

Carol looks at her. There is a moment — brief, like a gap between clouds — where Carol seems to inhabit herself rather than the framework.

Then the clouds close.

CAROL
I trust the process.

She says it the way people say *amen.*

Nadia writes something in her notebook.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY (SESSION 5)

Late afternoon light. The warmest the office gets.

This session has a different quality — McGee is sharper today. Alert in a way that wasn't there before.

McGEE
You seem different this week.

NADIA
Different how?

McGEE
More present. Less defended.

He's probing. He has seen something — some shift in her engagement — and he is trying to determine what it means.

NADIA
Maybe the process is working.

She says it lightly. He absorbs it without pleasure.

McGEE
Tell me about Marcus this week.

NADIA
I've been thinking about the last year. Patterns
I might have missed.

McGEE
What kind of patterns?

NADIA
He was seeing someone. A therapist. In your
referral network, actually.

A beat. Hairline thin.

McGEE
A lot of people come to me through referrals.

NADIA
Of course. I was just curious — do you have much
contact with the practitioners you refer to?

McGEE
Some. It depends.

NADIA
How do you decide who's in your network?

McGEE
(a gentle redirect)
Nadia. What's underneath this question?

She looks at him. And for the first time, she lets him see something real: the grief. Not performed, not tactical. She is genuinely sad, in this room, in this moment.

NADIA
I want to know if the person my brother was seeing
was helping him.

A long silence.

McGEE
(quietly, and he means this)
I don't know. I'm sorry.

And the terrible thing is: he does mean it. In this moment.

It doesn't change anything. But it's real.

Nadia nods. Looks at her hands.

McGEE (CONT'D)
I think there's something you're protecting yourself
from knowing. About your own role in Marcus's last year.

The grief hardens back into attention.

NADIA
What do you think I'm protecting myself from?

McGEE
That you saw something. And you decided to be busy.

The word *busy* again. Deliberate. Surgical.

Nadia picks up her notebook.

NADIA
(writing, not looking at him)
I'd like to come back to that.

CUT TO:

INT. NADIA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Her phone is recording.

She plays it back — tonight's session. Listens with her eyes closed.

When she gets to *"decided to be busy"* she stops it. Rewinds. Plays it again.

NADIA (V.O.)
(writing in her journal, we read it)
Phase 3 confirmed. Attribution now fully
internalized in the language. He is not
helping me process guilt. He is manufacturing
it. Specific, targeted, using the actual
circumstances of Marcus's death as material.

She pauses.

NADIA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The cruelty isn't incidental. It's structural.

She looks at the phone. At the recording.

She opens a new contact. Types: *DR. CAROL WHITMORE RECORDING — WITH CONSENT.*

CUT TO:

INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Nadia and Carol. Two coffees, a corner table.

Carol is here because Nadia told her she needed someone to talk to about the group. Carol is that kind of person — still, underneath the framework.

NADIA
How long have you been in the intensive?

CAROL
About seven months.

NADIA
Do you feel better than when you started?

Carol thinks about it. Really thinks.

CAROL
I feel like I understand myself better.

NADIA
That's not the same thing.

Carol looks up.

CAROL
Brian says —

NADIA
I know what Brian says.

(beat — gentler)

I'm asking what Carol says.

A long silence. Carol wraps her hands around her coffee cup.

CAROL
I'm tired.

It comes out smaller than she intended. Like something that's been waiting a long time to fit through a gap.

CAROL (CONT'D)
I'm just... very tired.

NADIA
I know.

(carefully)

Can I ask you something? And you can say no.

CAROL
Okay.

NADIA
Would you be willing to describe your experience
for me? In detail? On the record?

Carol looks at her.

CAROL
On the record for what?

NADIA
I don't know yet. But I want to have it. In case.

A very long pause. Carol looks out the window. A mother and child pass on the sidewalk outside.

CAROL
He's helped a lot of people.

NADIA
I know he has.

CAROL
It's not like it's been all bad.

NADIA
I know that too.

Carol turns back to her. And something in her face has settled — not resignation. A decision.

CAROL
Okay.

Nadia puts her phone on the table between them.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY (SESSION 6 — PRESENT DAY)

This is the last one.

Both of them know it, though neither has said so.

The light is different today — overcast outside, the warmth of the office more calculated than natural.

McGee is watching Nadia the way a chess player watches a board in the middle game — all the pieces visible, the question being which one moves first.

McGEE
How are you feeling?

NADIA
Clear.

McGEE
Clear is interesting. Tell me about clear.

NADIA
It's like — the noise gets quieter and you can
hear what's actually there.

McGEE
That's the work.

NADIA
Mm.

(beat)

Can I try something with you?

McGEE
Of course.

NADIA
A hypothetical. A thought experiment.

McGEE
Go ahead.

NADIA
Imagine a therapist. Brilliant, genuinely skilled —
the real deal. He can read people faster than almost
anyone. He knows, within twenty minutes, exactly
where the wound is.

McGee is very still.

NADIA (CONT'D)
And imagine that skill becomes the method. Not
helping patients close the wound — keeping it
accessible. Keeping them in the room.

McGEE
This sounds like a projection exercise.

NADIA
Humor me.

A beat.

McGEE
What's the question?

NADIA
Does that therapist know what he's doing?

McGee looks at her for a long moment.

McGEE
(slowly)
I think a person like that would have found a
way to believe that what they're doing is serving
the patient.

NADIA
Serving the patient. Or serving the process?

McGEE
In his mind, they're the same thing.

NADIA
And the patients who get worse?

McGEE
(a slight edge, now)
Resistance is part of any genuine process.

NADIA
Even when the resistance looks like someone
losing the will to be alive?

Silence.

McGEE
What are you actually asking me, Nadia?

She looks at him. And then she does something that surprises him: she agrees.

NADIA
You're right. I'm dancing around it.

She reaches into her bag. Sets her phone on the table between them — face down.

McGEE
(looking at it)
Is that recording?

NADIA
No.

(beat)

I want to ask you something directly.

McGEE
Then ask.

NADIA
Do you believe that instability — genuine
psychological instability — can be a productive
state for a patient?

McGEE
In certain phases, yes.

NADIA
And do you ever intentionally create that instability?

He looks at her for a very long time.

And then he does something that surprises her: he answers honestly.

McGEE
I create the conditions for transformation.
Transformation is inherently destabilizing. You
can't become something new while you're comfortable
with what you are.

NADIA
And the people who can't navigate the instability?

McGEE
(with genuine conviction)
They weren't ready. That's not a failure of the
process. That's data.

A long, terrible silence.

NADIA
(quietly)
They're people. Not data.

McGEE
Of course they are.

(a beat — softer, now, trying to find her)

Nadia. What happened to Marcus was not your fault.
And it was not a therapist's fault. Some people —

NADIA
Don't.

The word lands like a flat hand on a table.

McGEE
(very carefully)
You came here to find something to blame.

NADIA
I came here to understand something.

(beat)

And I do, now.

She picks up her phone from the table. Stands. Gets her coat from the hook by the door.

McGEE
If you're planning to —

NADIA
I'm not planning anything.

She buttons her coat. Turns back to look at him — just once.

NADIA (CONT'D)
You're very good at this. I want you to know
that I understand how good you are at this.

He absorbs that.

McGEE
(and despite everything, he means this too)
You're one of the most perceptive people I've
ever worked with.

NADIA
I know.

She walks out.

The door closes quietly.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

McGee sits in the chair.

The white noise machine hums.

He picks up his phone. Sets it down. Picks it up again.

He looks at the door she walked out of. At the chair she sat in.

His face does something it very rarely does: it loses its shape. Just for a moment. Just in the empty room.

Then it returns.

He dials.

McGEE
(into phone)
Clear my afternoon. I need to make some calls.

He stands. Goes to the window. Looks out.

The machine is already running.

FADE TO BLACK.

TITLE CARD: FLIPPED

TITLE CARD: *Dr. Brian McGee's podcast currently has 41 million listeners.*

TITLE CARD: *The average patient in his intensive program attends for 2.3 years.*

TITLE CARD: *There is no licensing board for "Integrative Reality Therapy."*

TITLE CARD: *It is not a recognized clinical method.*

FADE OUT.

THE END
